            ("How the analysis is computed", "The isobars come from a thin-plate spline through the 34 pressures: a smooth surface that passes through every station value, contoured with marching squares at 4 mb intervals and drawn only within about 230 km of a station. The cloud area is the 75 % contour of a Gaussian-weighted average of the cover (length scale 60 pixels, about 175 km); the precipitation is shaded where a similar weighted average of the rain stations exceeds 0.3. The fronts are placed by hand from the temperature, dew-point and wind contrasts in the table, so they are an interpretation, as they are on a real analysis chart."),
            ("A check on the wind", "Between stations 9 (1002.6 mb) and 5 (1008.8 mb), about 216 km apart, the pressure falls 6.2 mb. The geostrophic wind for that gradient is Δp ÷ (ρ f Δn) = 620 Pa ÷ (1.2 × 9.7 × 10^{-5} s^{-1} × 216,000 m) = 25 m/s, about 48 knots. The observed surface winds are 10 to 25 knots. The difference is what friction does to a surface wind (see {{p3-friction|Motion 8–11}}): it cuts the speed to a half or two thirds of the geostrophic wind."),
